```typescript
function isPrime(num: number): boolean {
    if (num < 2) return false;
    if (num === 2) return true;
    if (num % 2 === 0) return false;

    for (let i = 3; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i += 2) {
        if (num % i === 0) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

function main() {
    const n = parseInt(process.argv[2]);

    if (isNaN(n)) {
        return;
    }

    for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
        if (isPrime(i)) {
            console.log(i);
        }
    }
}

main();
```

This program works by:

1. Taking the command-line argument N and converting it to an integer
2. Defining a helper function `isPrime` that efficiently checks if a number is prime:
   - Numbers less than 2 are not prime
   - 2 is prime
   - Even numbers greater than 2 are not prime
   - For odd numbers, we only check divisibility up to the square root of the number, and only test odd divisors
3. Iterating from 2 to N and printing each prime number found

The program will output each prime number on a separate line, or nothing if there are no primes up to N.

